\description{
\code{SliceGeometry} represents the geometry of a rectangular 2D slice embedded in 3D
physical ("world") space: a starting point (\code{origin}), an orthonormal
in-plane basis (\code{direction_i}, \code{direction_j}), per-axis step sizes
(\code{spacing}), and per-axis sample counts (\code{size}). Sampling need not be
isotropic, and the slice need not be parallel to any Cartesian axis.

\code{SliceGeometry} holds no pixel/intensity data itself and has no dependency on any
particular image — it is pure geometry, analogous to how DICOM describes a
single slice (\code{ImagePositionPatient}/\code{ImageOrientationPatient}/
\code{PixelSpacing}) or how a SimpleITK image stores its own
origin/direction/spacing/size.

Construct a \code{SliceGeometry} from two opposite corners of the sampling rectangle.

Two opposite corners alone do not uniquely determine a rectangle in 3D:
for a fixed diagonal \code{p1 - p0}, any orthogonal decomposition of that
diagonal into two edge vectors gives a different, equally valid rectangle
(differing in orientation and, often, in which plane it lies on). Supplying
\code{direction_i} resolves this: the diagonal is projected onto \code{direction_i}
to get the extent along \code{i}, and \code{direction_j} is derived as the
(automatically orthogonal) remainder, which also fixes the plane.

Construct a \code{SliceGeometry} from its center point rather than its
\verb{(i = 0, j = 0)} corner.

Construct a \code{SliceGeometry} from a plane normal rather than an explicit
in-plane basis.

A normal alone does not fix the in-plane rotation (any rotation of
\code{direction_i}/\code{direction_j} about the normal is still a valid orthonormal
basis for the same plane). If \code{direction_i} is not supplied, a default seed
vector (the world x-axis, or the y-axis if the normal is nearly parallel to
x) is projected into the plane to pick one. If \code{direction_i} is supplied,
it is projected into the plane the same way, so it need not already be
exactly orthogonal to \code{normal}.

Construct a \code{SliceGeometry} matching an axis-aligned slice through an
existing 3D SimpleITK image — the geometric bridge to this package's
original \code{slice_axis()} semantics, generalized to an oblique image
direction matrix.

The in-plane axes, their spacing, and their sample counts are taken
directly from \code{image}'s own geometry (the two axes other than \code{axis}); the
out-of-plane position is snapped to the nearest voxel plane to
\code{coordinate} along \code{axis}, exactly as the legacy \code{slice_axis()} did.

Construct a \code{SliceGeometry} from the 4 labeled corners produced by
\code{get_bounds()} — the inverse of that getter. Unlike \code{from_corners()}, no
extra disambiguating direction is needed: 4 labeled corners fully
determine the rectangle (\code{direction_i}/\code{direction_j} come directly from
two of its edges).
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# A 10x10 axial slice, 1mm spacing, centered at the world origin
geom <- SliceGeometry$new(
  origin = c(-5, -5, 0),
  direction_i = c(1, 0, 0),
  direction_j = c(0, 1, 0),
  spacing = c(1, 1),
  size = c(10, 10)
)
geom$get_extent()
geom$get_sample_points()
}

\dontrun{
geom <- SliceGeometry$from_corners(
  p0 = c(0, 0, 0), p1 = c(10, 5, 0),
  direction_i = c(1, 0, 0), size = c(11, 6)
)
}
\dontrun{
geom <- SliceGeometry$from_center(
  center = c(0, 0, 0),
  direction_i = c(1, 0, 0), direction_j = c(0, 1, 0),
  spacing = c(1, 1), size = c(10, 10)
)
}
\dontrun{
# A coronal-like plane through the origin, normal to the y-axis
geom <- SliceGeometry$from_normal(
  origin = c(0, 0, 0), normal = c(0, 1, 0),
  spacing = c(1, 1), size = c(10, 10)
)
}
